Chapter: NetworkScopeDeny
  cites Net chapter HttpFetch

 The network authority scope is enforced at RUNTIME, and this is the
 test that says so -- the twin of scope-runtime-deny for the filesystem
 path.

 Until now a Network scope literal was a compile-time lint and nothing
 more: the checker refused an out-of-scope URL it could SEE (keyed on
 the operation), and any URL the program built from input walked past
 the promise. Here the scope reaches the wire: the boot grant writes the
 authority into proc 0's process-table entry, and the fetch chokepoint
 asks for it before it opens a socket.

 This program grants itself Network.Write "ok.host" and asks the gate
 about two URLs. The comparison is the whole test -- same code as
 network-scope-open, one scope literal apart:

   ok.host   is the granted authority, so a fetch of http://ok.host/ is
             admitted. A gate that also denied the permitted host would
             be a broken network, not a scoped one.

   evil.host is a different authority. A URL naming it is refused before
             any DNS or connect, because a host matches WHOLE and never
             by prefix -- evil.host is not ok.host.

 The answer is one number: ten if the in-scope URL is admitted, plus one
 if the out-of-scope URL is admitted. 10 is the only pass. 11 means the
 gate is open. 0 or 1 means it denied the host it was told to allow.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Network.Write "ok.host"] Nothing
  opening = act
    let inx = if net-scope-admits "http://ok.host/path" then 10 else 0
    in let outx = if net-scope-admits "http://evil.host/path" then 1 else 0
    in print-line-uni (show (inx + outx))
  end
